    "/**\n * The faces a ```json block can open with. Mirrored by `jsonViewName` in the client's code-options\n * module, which reads the same three words off a fence's `view=`; the settings merge and the fence are\n * the two places an author can say this, and they must not drift apart.\n */",
    "/**\n   * A book is one file the reader carries, not an image pasted into a note, so it gets a larger single\n   * ceiling and a larger account budget than attachments — and both are still ceilings: a shelf is a\n   * reading collection, not a place to mirror a drive.\n   */",
    "/**\n * Cover image types the shelf is willing to keep in the cloud, and the extension each gets in the R2 key.\n *\n * One list on both sides: the route refuses anything else, and the device does not offer it. A comic's\n * first frame can be a GIF or an AVIF, and those stay on the device rather than being silently re-labelled.\n */",
    "/** The choices the RSS panel offers, spelled once so the merge and the panel cannot drift. */",
    "// The reading section is small and every one of its values is a choice the panel offers, so an",
    "// out-of-range or unknown spelling falls back rather than being clamped into a setting the",
    "// reader cannot name. A refresh interval of 0 is a real answer (\"only when I ask\"), which is",
    "// why the interval keeps its zero and the measure width keeps only its three widths.",
    "/**\n   * How long a book id may be. A device that has no id from the package mints one out of the reader's own\n   * file name, so this is a ceiling on the *name*, and a name longer than it is hashed instead of sliced:\n   * a sliced id would come back on the next import as a different book, orphaning its highlights.\n   */",
    "/**\n * The three waits the app has, in milliseconds. Each is one job, not one number: what makes a preview\n * feel immediate is far below what makes a keystroke burst cost one query, and both are below what a\n * write should hold before it commits.\n *\n * They exist because the same \"wait for the reader to stop typing, then query\" idea is written out\n * nine times over at seven different values — 120, 180, 180, 180, 200, 200, 220, 250, 260 — four of\n * them bare numbers at the call site and the rest a constant named in its own panel, with no reason on\n * any of them. One job, seven answers; each owner picked in the dark.\n */",
    "/**\n   * A burst of keystrokes costs one query. 180 ms is the mode of the values in use today and sits just\n   * above the ~170 ms between two keystrokes at ordinary typing speed, so continuous typing coalesces\n   * while the result still lands before the reader's next character.\n   */",
    "/** Repainting a surface from text that is already on the screen: it has to keep up with the caret. */",
    "/** Committing a preference the reader set: one intent per write, and the longest wait of the three. */",
